1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,984
In 1477, outside the gates of the Joseon capital, a

2
00:00:03,984 --> 00:00:06,796
group of shamans performed a ritual.

3
00:00:06,796 --> 00:00:08,359
Someone reported it.

4
00:00:08,359 --> 00:00:11,093
The court asked who had ordered it.

5
00:00:11,093 --> 00:00:13,202
The answer: the palace had.

6
00:00:13,202 --> 00:00:17,264
The king's own wet nurse and a royal eunuch had gone

7
00:00:17,264 --> 00:00:18,358
there to pray.

8
00:00:18,358 --> 00:00:22,576
The king's response was a single line, recorded in the

9
00:00:22,576 --> 00:00:24,998
Annals: "Do not do this again."

10
00:00:24,998 --> 00:00:28,904
That is the whole story of Joseon in one exchange.

11
00:00:28,904 --> 00:00:33,044
For five hundred years, this Confucian state outlawed

12
00:00:33,044 --> 00:00:37,419
shamanism, taxed it, exiled its practitioners, and wrote

13
00:00:37,419 --> 00:00:38,669
laws against it.

14
00:00:38,669 --> 00:00:43,512
And for five hundred years, the palace kept calling them back.

15
00:00:44,062 --> 00:00:47,842
Korean shamanism, musok, is older than Buddhism,

16
00:00:47,842 --> 00:00:50,913
Confucianism, or Christianity in Korea.

17
00:00:50,913 --> 00:00:55,008
Its practitioners are the mudang, usually women, who

18
00:00:55,008 --> 00:00:58,788
speak for spirits and perform the gut: drumming,

19
00:00:58,788 --> 00:01:01,859
dancing, and negotiation with the dead.

20
00:01:01,859 --> 00:01:06,269
The Joseon dynasty was built on Neo-Confucianism, and it

21
00:01:06,269 --> 00:01:10,443
saw all of this as eumsa. Lewd rites. Not just wrong.

22
00:01:10,443 --> 00:01:11,343
Disorderly.

23
00:01:11,343 --> 00:01:15,437
In 1431, the Censorate petitioned King Sejong with a

24
00:01:15,437 --> 00:01:18,587
complaint that sounds remarkably modern.

25
00:01:18,587 --> 00:01:22,840
Shamans were living inside the capital, and noblewomen

26
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:25,202
were visiting them constantly.

27
00:01:25,202 --> 00:01:28,982
Sick people were being gathered in their houses,

28
00:01:28,982 --> 00:01:32,447
spreading disease through the neighborhoods.

29
00:01:32,447 --> 00:01:35,125
The fix was simple. Move them out.

30
00:01:35,125 --> 00:01:38,826
Shamans were pushed beyond the city walls, into

31
00:01:38,826 --> 00:01:43,157
settlements that the records literally call mugyeok-ri.

32
00:01:43,157 --> 00:01:44,417
Shaman villages.

33
00:01:44,417 --> 00:01:48,748
The most famous stood outside the South Gate, and along

34
00:01:48,748 --> 00:01:50,953
the Han River at Noryangjin.

35
00:01:50,953 --> 00:01:54,418
They were also pushed down the social order.

36
00:01:54,418 --> 00:01:57,174
Shamans were classified as sacheon.

37
00:01:57,174 --> 00:02:01,506
Lowborn, ranked alongside butchers, actors, and slaves.

38
00:02:01,506 --> 00:02:05,679
In the early dynasty, they and their descendants were

39
00:02:05,679 --> 00:02:08,278
barred from receiving state land.

40
00:02:08,828 --> 00:02:12,557
Here is where the story stops being a simple ban.

41
00:02:12,557 --> 00:02:14,915
The palace had its own shamans.

42
00:02:14,915 --> 00:02:18,948
They were called gungmu, state shamans, and they were

43
00:02:18,948 --> 00:02:22,753
housed in an office inside the palace grounds: the

44
00:02:22,753 --> 00:02:25,112
Seongsucheong. The Star Shrine.

45
00:02:25,112 --> 00:02:29,297
Their job was to pray for the royal family and to avert

46
00:02:29,297 --> 00:02:31,732
disaster. This was not a secret.

47
00:02:31,732 --> 00:02:35,460
It was an institution, with a budget and a staff.

48
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:40,254
And when the country needed rain, the state called the shamans.

49
00:02:40,254 --> 00:02:43,983
In 1410, King Taejong gathered seventy shamans at

50
00:02:43,983 --> 00:02:46,037
Baegaksan to pray for rain.

51
00:02:46,037 --> 00:02:50,146
In 1436, King Sejong gave shamans rice to perform rain

52
00:02:50,146 --> 00:02:53,723
rites, and the record notes that the rain came.

53
00:02:53,723 --> 00:02:57,527
Again and again, the Annals show the same pattern.

54
00:02:57,527 --> 00:03:01,256
The Confucian state condemning shamanic ritual in

55
00:03:01,256 --> 00:03:04,071
public, and requesting it in private.

56
00:03:04,071 --> 00:03:07,419
The Star Shrine survived for over a century.

57
00:03:07,419 --> 00:03:11,072
In 1503, King Yeonsangun, no friend of Confucian

58
00:03:11,072 --> 00:03:14,876
officials, stated plainly that the office of state

59
00:03:14,876 --> 00:03:17,996
shamans was ancient, and should continue.

60
00:03:18,546 --> 00:03:21,553
In 1478, King Seongjong lost patience.

61
00:03:21,553 --> 00:03:25,985
The shamans had been expelled from the capital, and then

62
00:03:25,985 --> 00:03:28,043
they had simply come back.

63
00:03:28,043 --> 00:03:30,338
The prohibition had loosened.

64
00:03:30,338 --> 00:03:34,691
They were practicing in the city again, openly, without

65
00:03:34,691 --> 00:03:36,669
the slightest hesitation.

66
00:03:36,669 --> 00:03:41,022
His order to the Censorate is one of the most revealing

67
00:03:41,022 --> 00:03:42,605
lines in the Annals.

68
00:03:42,605 --> 00:03:44,662
"It has become the custom.

69
00:03:44,662 --> 00:03:46,562
The lewd rites flourish.

70
00:03:46,562 --> 00:03:49,253
The ignorant crowd flocks to them.

71
00:03:49,253 --> 00:03:51,785
It cannot be left unprohibited."

72
00:03:51,785 --> 00:03:53,051
Read that again.

73
00:03:53,051 --> 00:03:57,483
It is the sound of a state admitting that its law is not

74
00:03:57,483 --> 00:03:58,380
working.

75
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:00,280
So the laws got harsher.

76
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:04,078
Shamans caught inside the capital were punished.

77
00:04:04,078 --> 00:04:08,115
Officials who failed to arrest them were dismissed.

78
00:04:08,115 --> 00:04:12,230
And the state began to tax them, the muse, partly to

79
00:04:12,230 --> 00:04:16,504
squeeze them out of business, partly because it needed

80
00:04:16,504 --> 00:04:21,410
the money. In 1464, the government ordered a register of every

81
00:04:21,410 --> 00:04:25,763
shaman, compiled every three years, so the tax could be

82
00:04:25,763 --> 00:04:27,267
collected properly.

83
00:04:27,267 --> 00:04:29,562
Later records list the rates.

84
00:04:29,562 --> 00:04:32,569
The chief state shaman paid nine geun.

85
00:04:32,569 --> 00:04:34,785
An ordinary shaman paid one.

86
00:04:34,785 --> 00:04:39,613
And yet. In 1684, a shaman named Makrye was caught inside the

87
00:04:39,613 --> 00:04:43,886
palace, performing rituals, and wearing the king's own

88
00:04:43,886 --> 00:04:48,081
dragon robe. She was sentenced to exile on an island.

89
00:04:48,081 --> 00:04:52,354
In 1743, King Yeongjo signed a law ordering any shaman

90
00:04:52,354 --> 00:04:56,707
who entered the palace in royal robes to be banished to

91
00:04:56,707 --> 00:04:57,973
a remote island.

92
00:04:57,973 --> 00:05:00,981
By then, the pattern was unmistakable.

93
00:05:00,981 --> 00:05:05,729
Every generation passed a new law against the same practice.

94
00:05:05,729 --> 00:05:10,161
You do not need a new law for something that has stopped

95
00:05:10,161 --> 00:05:11,058
happening.

96
00:05:11,608 --> 00:05:15,360
Because the ban was aimed at the wrong target.

97
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:19,194
The state could legislate where a shaman lived.

98
00:05:19,194 --> 00:05:22,946
It could not legislate why people went to one.

99
00:05:22,946 --> 00:05:27,677
Consider what the 1431 petition actually complained about.

100
00:05:27,677 --> 00:05:31,593
Noblewomen visiting shaman houses. Not peasants.

101
00:05:31,593 --> 00:05:33,143
Not the uneducated.

102
00:05:33,143 --> 00:05:37,547
The wives of the elite, slipping out of the capital to

103
00:05:37,547 --> 00:05:41,952
ask about a sick child, a difficult birth, a husband's

104
00:05:41,952 --> 00:05:43,747
career, a dead parent.

105
00:05:43,747 --> 00:05:46,031
The Annals are full of this.

106
00:05:46,031 --> 00:05:50,843
A queen dowager calls for a shaman when the king falls ill.

107
00:05:50,843 --> 00:05:53,454
A royal concubine sends for one.

108
00:05:53,454 --> 00:05:57,532
A crown prince's illness becomes a shamanic ritual

109
00:05:57,532 --> 00:06:00,061
conducted by the palace itself.

110
00:06:00,061 --> 00:06:04,955
And there was a practical reason shamans were hard to erase.

111
00:06:04,955 --> 00:06:07,728
They were also the medical system.

112
00:06:07,728 --> 00:06:12,215
The state assigned shamans to the Hwalinseo, the public

113
00:06:12,215 --> 00:06:16,130
clinic for the poor, where they treated patients

114
00:06:16,130 --> 00:06:17,843
alongside physicians.

115
00:06:17,843 --> 00:06:20,535
The tax they paid helped fund it.

116
00:06:20,535 --> 00:06:24,858
For people with no access to a doctor, the shaman was

117
00:06:24,858 --> 00:06:26,245
not superstition.

118
00:06:26,245 --> 00:06:28,855
She was the only help available.

119
00:06:28,855 --> 00:06:32,281
Grief, illness, infertility, sudden death.

120
00:06:32,281 --> 00:06:36,604
The things a Confucian bureaucracy had no ritual for.

121
00:06:36,604 --> 00:06:39,785
Shamanism had a ritual for all of them.

122
00:06:39,785 --> 00:06:41,906
You can outlaw a practice.

123
00:06:41,906 --> 00:06:43,864
You cannot outlaw grief.

124
00:06:44,414 --> 00:06:49,321
By the eighteenth century, the state had stopped trying to win.

125
00:06:49,321 --> 00:06:53,449
In 1745, Yeongjo formally abolished the last official

126
00:06:53,449 --> 00:06:57,578
shamanic rites from the register of state ceremonies.

127
00:06:57,578 --> 00:07:01,550
In 1763, the Censorate asked once more to expel the

128
00:07:01,550 --> 00:07:04,666
shamans from Seoul, and the king agreed.

129
00:07:04,666 --> 00:07:06,223
And still they came.

130
00:07:06,223 --> 00:07:10,430
In the final decades of the dynasty, the most powerful

131
00:07:10,430 --> 00:07:13,935
woman in Korea, Queen Min, the future Empress

132
00:07:13,935 --> 00:07:18,219
Myeongseong, kept shamans close enough that two of them

133
00:07:18,219 --> 00:07:20,945
were granted honorary noble titles.

134
00:07:20,945 --> 00:07:24,839
When Japan annexed Korea in 1910, the new colonial

135
00:07:24,839 --> 00:07:28,578
government tried again, classifying shamanism as

136
00:07:28,578 --> 00:07:30,370
primitive superstition.

137
00:07:30,370 --> 00:07:34,731
Then the military governments of the 1960s and seventies

138
00:07:34,731 --> 00:07:39,327
tried again, shutting down shrines and calling it backward.

139
00:07:39,327 --> 00:07:41,430
Each time, the same result.

140
00:07:41,430 --> 00:07:43,923
Today, shamanism is not a relic.

141
00:07:43,923 --> 00:07:46,026
It is a working profession.

142
00:07:46,026 --> 00:07:50,232
Mudangs counsel clients, perform gut for the dead, and

143
00:07:50,232 --> 00:07:51,867
appear on television.

144
00:07:51,867 --> 00:07:55,918
Estimates of their number range wildly, from tens of

145
00:07:55,918 --> 00:07:58,644
thousands to hundreds of thousands.

146
00:07:58,644 --> 00:08:01,604
But the more telling fact is cultural.

147
00:08:01,604 --> 00:08:05,966
When a Korean film wants to talk about grief, guilt, and

148
00:08:05,966 --> 00:08:09,471
the unburied dead, it reaches for the shaman.

149
00:08:09,471 --> 00:08:11,106
The Wailing, in 2016.

150
00:08:11,106 --> 00:08:12,353
Exhuma, in 2024.

151
00:08:12,353 --> 00:08:16,559
Both films are built on concepts that the Joseon state

152
00:08:16,559 --> 00:08:19,752
spent five hundred years trying to erase.

153
00:08:19,752 --> 00:08:22,011
Sal, spiritual contamination.

154
00:08:22,011 --> 00:08:24,036
Wonhon, the restless dead.

155
00:08:24,036 --> 00:08:27,931
And sinbyeong, the spirit sickness that is said to

156
00:08:27,931 --> 00:08:31,358
choose a shaman whether she wants it or not.

157
00:08:31,908 --> 00:08:36,935
Which brings us back to the thing the laws could never touch.

158
00:08:36,935 --> 00:08:41,632
In the tradition, a mudang does not decide to become one.

159
00:08:41,632 --> 00:08:43,198
The spirits decide.

160
00:08:43,198 --> 00:08:45,011
The sign is sinbyeong.

161
00:08:45,011 --> 00:08:49,791
Unexplained illness, seizures, hallucinations, nightmares.

162
00:08:49,791 --> 00:08:52,428
A body that medicine cannot fix.

163
00:08:52,428 --> 00:08:56,796
The cure is naerim, an initiation ritual in which the

164
00:08:56,796 --> 00:08:58,939
person accepts the spirit.

165
00:08:58,939 --> 00:09:03,224
Refuse, the tradition says, and the illness worsens.

166
00:09:03,224 --> 00:09:05,696
Some accounts say people died.

167
00:09:05,696 --> 00:09:10,064
Modern psychiatry has another name for parts of this.

168
00:09:10,064 --> 00:09:11,795
It also has patients.

169
00:09:11,795 --> 00:09:16,327
That tension, illness or calling, diagnosis or destiny,

170
00:09:16,327 --> 00:09:18,965
is why the story never resolves.

171
00:09:18,965 --> 00:09:22,591
The state could classify shamans as lowborn.

172
00:09:22,591 --> 00:09:26,876
It could tax them, exile them, and write them out of

173
00:09:26,876 --> 00:09:28,359
official ceremony.

174
00:09:28,359 --> 00:09:32,892
It could not answer the question they were being asked.

175
00:09:33,442 --> 00:09:35,435
Five hundred years of laws.

176
00:09:35,435 --> 00:09:39,349
Five hundred years of the same ritual, performed just

177
00:09:39,349 --> 00:09:40,604
outside the gate.

178
00:09:40,604 --> 00:09:44,517
The kings of Joseon were not fighting a superstition.

179
00:09:44,517 --> 00:09:48,282
They were fighting the fact that, in the dark, when

180
00:09:48,282 --> 00:09:52,269
someone is dying or gone, people want someone to speak

181
00:09:52,269 --> 00:09:53,598
to the other side.

182
00:09:53,598 --> 00:09:55,001
The state had laws.

183
00:09:55,001 --> 00:09:56,920
The shamans had an answer.

184
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:00,907
If you want more of the records the official histories

185
00:10:00,907 --> 00:10:02,753
tried to bury, subscribe.

186
00:10:02,753 --> 00:10:06,740
Next: the king who drove the Confucian scholars out of

187
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:10,210
the palace, and filled it with shamans instead.

